  "narrative": {
    "intro": "FY2026 to date the federal government has obligated $30.8B for Facilities Support Services under NAICS 561210. Obligations fell 16.9% year over year. There have been 11,164 awards so far, with an average award size of $2.8M. Spending has trended down from a recent peak: FY24 was $37.5B and FY25 was $37.1B, then FY26 to date is $30.8B. The Department of Energy is the dominant buyer with $23.2B in obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense follows with $4.8B. Other top buyers include the General Services Administration with $553M, the Department of Homeland Security with $447M, and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $345M. On the supplier side a handful of large vendors account for the biggest shares of obligations. TRIAD NATIONAL SECURITY, LLC received $5.1B, NATIONAL TECHNOLOGY & ENGINEERING SOLUTIONS OF SANDIA, LLC received $4.5B, and SAVANNAH RIVER NUCLEAR SOLUTIONS LLC received $3.3B in FY2026 to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is FY2026 to date spending in this market?",
        "body": "Spending is highly concentrated. The Department of Energy accounts for the largest portion with $23.2B in obligations in FY2026 to date. The Department of Defense follows with $4.8B. The next largest buyers are the General Services Administration with $553M, the Department of Homeland Security with $447M, and the National Aeronautics and Space Administration with $345M."
      },
      {
        "heading": "What do award count and award size tell contractors?",
        "body": "There were 11,164 awards in FY2026 to date with an average award size of $2.8M. That combination indicates many mid-sized awards rather than a handful of very small or only very large awards, even though a few vendors have multi-billion-dollar totals in FY2026 to date."
      }
    ]
